{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 9,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.046,
            0.364,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.087,
            0.588,
            0.144
          ],
          "content": "Then, we multiply this result by 5 to get the product. This is $\\frac { ( 5 \\times 7 ) } { ( 1 2 \\times 1 8 ) } \\cdot$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.148,
            0.442,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{5}{12} \\times \\frac{7}{8} = \\frac{(5 \\times 7)}{(12 \\times 18)} = \\frac{35}{216}",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.195,
            0.549,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": "From this we can see that, in general,"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.602,
            0.087,
            0.841,
            0.239
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.301,
            0.228,
            0.437,
            0.263
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{a}{b} \\times \\frac{c}{d} = \\frac{a \\times c}{b \\times d}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.269,
            0.847,
            0.307
          ],
          "content": "This formula was first stated in this general form by Brahmagupta in his Brāhmasphuṭasiddhānta in 628 CE."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.306,
            0.849,
            0.363
          ],
          "content": "The formula above works even when the multiplier or multiplicand fraction with denominator 1. For example,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.369,
            0.372,
            0.63,
            0.58
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{3 \\times \\frac{3}{4}  can be written  \\frac{3}{1} \\times \\frac{3}{4}}{= \\frac{3 \\times 3}{1 \\times 4} = \\frac{9}{4}.}",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.592,
            0.847,
            0.616
          ],
          "content": "**Multiplication of Fractions — Simplifying to Lowest Form**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.622,
            0.847,
            0.662
          ],
          "content": "？ Multiply the following fractions and express the product in its lowest form:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.459,
            0.673,
            0.54,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": "\\frac { 1 2 } { 7 } \\times \\frac { 5 } { 2 4 }",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.717,
            0.847,
            0.757
          ],
          "content": "Instead of multiplying the numerators (12 and 5) and denominators (7 and 24) first and then simplifying, we could do the following:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.418,
            0.763,
            0.58,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{12}{7} \\times \\frac{5}{24} = \\frac{\\textcircled{1} \\times 5}{7 \\times \\textcircled{2}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.813,
            0.848,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "We see that both the circled numbers have a common factor of 12. We know that a fraction remains the same when the numerator and denominator are divided by the common factor. In this case, we can divide them by 12."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.938,
            0.193,
            0.955
          ],
          "content": "182"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
